index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..4506bd95bc6105cef3e39fd33232f17a3862c932
--- /dev/null
+++ b/experiments/002-living-map/generate/m2/specs/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+# M2 adventurous target synthesis
+
+Approved by Dex on 22 September 2026. These specifications read the five adventurous boards as design targets and the five keyless plates as geometry truth. The boards are strongest on material, roof programme, planting maturity, water and atmosphere. They are not reliable surveys. Several regenerated roads, ponds, woodland edges and block dimensions drift by more than the intended 15 pixels, especially in `scheme-a` and `campus-b`, and must not be copied into the live model.
+
+## Shared palette
+
+| Use | Lit sRGB | Shaded sRGB | Target reading |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| Buff brick and mineral masonry | `#BE986C` | `#72563E` | Warm, varied and slightly weathered, never orange |
+| Honey mass timber | `#C49A65` | `#6D5038` | Expressed posts and floor edges with real depth |
+| Pale stone or concrete | `#C7B79A` | `#817562` | Calm annexes, parapets and occasional bands |
+| Reflective glazing | `#687A75` | `#263B3B` | Opaque dark reflection, not transparent black holes |
+| Bronze trim and plinth | `#665747` | `#302C27` | A restrained secondary line |
+| PV glass | `#17242E` | `#101820` | Sparse glint `#91A4AB`, never cyan |
+| Meadow roof | `#8D9552` | `#4A542F` | Late-summer green with straw, yellow, white and muted purple fleck |
+| Buff hard landscape | `#A69A83` | `#70695B` | Setts or resin-bound paths distinct from asphalt |
+| Asphalt | `#60615C` | `#3E403C` | Worn and low contrast |
+| Open water | `#8CA5A9` | `#2E4B4F` | Broken sky reflection with reed-darkened edges |
+
+## Roof programme
+
+Area-weighted target across the five views: **PV 40%, meadow or green 29%, slate or tile 22%, walkable terrace 6%, other 3%**. Apply by typology, not as a random global mix. New pitched homes should approach whole-roof PV on their favourable planes. Campus roofs should alternate approximately equal planted and PV zones, with readable maintenance or public paths. Taller residential blocks should be mostly meadow with limited terraces. GCHQ remains the dominant meadow roof and its car parks carry elevated PV canopies. Existing Cheltenham remains predominantly slate and clay tile.
+
+Fine flower species, furniture and detailed balustrades will not resolve from 340 to 450 m. Use shader-scale variation, clear path widths, parapet silhouettes and shadow. The board's occasional roof landforms that appear to run beyond the plate envelope are not buildable within the locked massing and must be omitted.
+
+## Tree and ground palette
+
+Use five principal tree forms: broad veteran oak at 17 to 23 m, irregular mixed woodland at 11 to 19 m, upright lime or hornbeam avenue trees at 9 to 15 m, orchard trees at 4 to 7 m, and multi-stem willow or alder at 7 to 12 m. Hedges are 1.8 to 3 m. A shared late-summer canopy range is lit `#70833E`, mid `#526B34`, shaded `#253D24`; orchard crowns can be slightly lighter and woodland interiors slightly bluer and darker. The visual rule is arrangement as much as shape: clumped woodland, disciplined avenues, regular orchards, isolated veterans and low wetland species. Avoid equal spacing and a single crown silhouette.
+
+The ground range is meadow `#8FA064`, pasture `#7E925F`, wet meadow `#789067`, garden planting `#667B45`, deep woodland floor `#465A39`, buff path `#A69A83`, asphalt `#60615C` and water `#8CA5A9` lit or `#2E4B4F` shaded. Developed parcels should read as gardens, planted courts and rain gardens rather than generic lawn. Land-cover edges need metre-scale softness and broad tonal variation, not high-frequency noise.
+
+## Top modelling actions by visible impact per effort
+
+| Rank | Action | Main system | Effort | M5 status |
+|---:|---|---|---:|---|
+| 1 | Replace the shared faceted tree with five readable crown families, then use clumps, avenues, orchards and veterans rather than even scatter | `landcover.js`, `future.js`, `gv-2045-trees.bin` generation | 1.5 to 2 days | **Covered by in-flight M5** |
+| 2 | Separate parcel ground into meadow, gardens, buff courts, wetland and road, soften boundaries and add broad colour variation | `scripts/golden_valley_2045.py`, generated land cover, `future.js` | 1 to 1.5 days | **Covered by in-flight M5** |
+| 3 | Make mapped channels and two or three ponded reaches reflect the sky, with soft reed margins; keep the surveyed brook narrow | generated land cover, `future.js`, `life.js` | 1 day | **Covered by in-flight M5** |
+| 4 | Replace painted agrivoltaic stripes with instanced 2 m vertical bifacial rows at about 11 m centres, terrain-following with narrow shadows | `scripts/golden_valley_2045.py`, `futureGeometry` | 1 to 1.5 days | M6, not M5 |
+| 5 | Tune roof allocation to the 40/29/22/6/3 target and strengthen PV seams, meadow variation and walkable path masks at aerial distance | `facades.js`, `future.js`, generator data | 1 day | M4 follow-up |
+| 6 | Deepen campus facade reveals and expressed timber bays, and split domestic masonry from campus material rules | `facades.js`, `futureGeometry` in `future.js` | 1 day | M4 follow-up |
+| 7 | Make GCHQ solar canopies visibly elevated with slim posts, lighter undersides and readable gaps over parked surfaces | `scripts/golden_valley_2045.py`, `futureGeometry` | 0.5 to 1 day | M4 follow-up |
+| 8 | Upgrade the GCHQ ring facade from a smooth cylinder to fine vertical timber or bronze bays over dark reflective glass | `models.js` or a dedicated GCHQ material branch | 1 day | Not in M5 |
+| 9 | Make glasshouses read as framed glass volumes through pane and frame contrast, without fragile true transparency | glasshouse branch in `futureGeometry` and `facades.js` | 0.5 day | M6 |
+| 10 | Add people, sheep and vehicles only if M7 tests still lack scale cues, using sparse stable instances or sprites | optional `life.js` extension | 0.5 day | Deferred, outside current aerial scope |
+
+M5 already addresses the three highest-value landscape deficiencies: crown variety and maturity, the flat and hard-edged ground classes, and water that currently behaves like coloured terrain. It should not absorb the agrivoltaic, glasshouse, facade or roof work. Those are separate object and material tasks.
+
+The approved boards contain photographic leaf detail, tiny occupants and locally invented drainage geometry that the live WebGL model cannot honestly reproduce at this range and within its performance budget. The right target is the same hierarchy at first glance: mature spatial tree mass, distinct productive landscapes, readable roof programmes, deep warm facades, narrow reflective water and credible shadows. Geometry stays with the plates.
